</ul><p>Removes a header that's already defined into headers object.</p><div class="codeblock"><p class="codelabel">Code example:</p><pre><code>request.removeHeader('Content-Type');</code></pre><p class="codeend">End of code.</p></div></section>
<section><h3 id="requestreusedsocket"><code>request.reusedSocket</code><a id="http_request_reusedsocket"></a></h3><div class="api_metadata">Added in: v13.0.0, v12.16.0</div><ul>
<li>Type: <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/docs/Web/JavaScript/Data_structures#boolean_type">boolean</a>. Whether the request is sent through a reused socket.</li>
</ul><p>When sending a request through a keep-alive-enabled agent, the underlying socket
might be reused. If the server closes the connection at the same time that the
agent reuses it, the request may encounter an <code>ECONNRESET</code> error.</p><p>The following example sends requests near the server's keep-alive timeout to
make this race easier to reproduce. It sets
<a href="#serverkeepalivetimeoutbuffer"><code>server.keepAliveTimeoutBuffer</code></a> to <code>0</code> so the internal socket timeout
matches <a href="#http--serverkeepalivetimeout"><code>server.keepAliveTimeout</code></a>.</p><div class="codeblock"><p class="codelabel">ES module version:</p><pre><code>import http from 'node:http';
const agent = new http.Agent({ keepAlive: true });
const server = http.createServer(
  { keepAliveTimeout: 3000, keepAliveTimeoutBuffer: 0 },
  (req, res) => {
    res.write('hello\n');
    res.end();
  },
);
server.listen(3000);

setInterval(() => {
  http.get('http://localhost:3000', { agent }, (res) => {
    res.resume();
  });
}, 3000);</code></pre><p class="codeend">End of code.</p><p class="codelabel">CommonJS version:</p><pre><code>const http = require('node:http');
const agent = new http.Agent({ keepAlive: true });
const server = http.createServer(
  { keepAliveTimeout: 3000, keepAliveTimeoutBuffer: 0 },
  (req, res) => {
    res.write('hello\n');
    res.end();
  },
);
server.listen(3000);

setInterval(() => {
  http.get('http://localhost:3000', { agent }, (res) => {
    res.resume();
  });
}, 3000);</code></pre><p class="codeend">End of code.</p></div><p>By checking <code>request.reusedSocket</code>, an application can retry idempotent requests
that fail because a reused connection was reset.</p><div class="codeblock"><p class="codelabel">ES module version:</p><pre><code>import http from 'node:http';
const agent = new http.Agent({ keepAlive: true });

function retriableRequest() {
  const req = http
    .get('http://localhost:3000', { agent }, (res) => {
      // ...
    })
    .on('error', (err) => {
      // Check if retry is needed
      if (req.reusedSocket && err.code === 'ECONNRESET') {
        retriableRequest();
      }
    });
}

retriableRequest();</code></pre><p class="codeend">End of code.</p><p class="codelabel">CommonJS version:</p><pre><code>const http = require('node:http');
const agent = new http.Agent({ keepAlive: true });

function retriableRequest() {
  const req = http
    .get('http://localhost:3000', { agent }, (res) => {
      // ...
    })
    .on('error', (err) => {
      // Check if retry is needed
      if (req.reusedSocket && err.code === 'ECONNRESET') {
        retriableRequest();
      }
    });
}

retriableRequest();</code></pre><p class="codeend">End of code.</p></div></section>
<section><h3 id="requestsetheadername-value"><code>request.setHeader(name, value)</code><a id="http_request_setheader_name_value"></a></h3><div class="api_metadata">Added in: v1.6.0</div><ul>
<li><code>name</code> (<a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/docs/Web/JavaScript/Data_structures#string_type">string</a>)</li>
<li><code>value</code> (<a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/docs/Web/JavaScript/Data_structures#Data_types">any</a>)</li>
</ul><p>Sets a single header value for headers object. If this header already exists in
the to-be-sent headers, its value will be replaced. Use an array of strings
here to send multiple headers with the same name. Non-string values will be
stored without modification. Therefore, <a href="#requestgetheadername"><code>request.getHeader()</code></a> may return
non-string values. However, the non-string values will be converted to strings
for network transmission.</p><div class="codeblock"><p class="codelabel">Code example:</p><pre><code>request.setHeader('Content-Type', 'application/json');</code></pre><p class="codeend">End of code.</p></div><p>or</p><div class="codeblock"><p class="codelabel">Code example:</p><pre><code>request.setHeader('Cookie', ['type=ninja', 'language=javascript']);</code></pre><p class="codeend">End of code.</p></div><p>When the value is a string an exception will be thrown if it contains
characters outside the <code>latin1</code> encoding.</p><p>If you need to pass UTF-8 characters in the value please encode the value
using the <a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8187.txt">RFC 8187</a> standard.</p><div class="codeblock"><p class="codelabel">Code example:</p><pre><code>const filename = 'Rock 🎵.txt';
request.setHeader('Content-Disposition', `attachment; filename*=utf-8''${encodeURIComponent(filename)}`);</code></pre><p class="codeend">End of code.</p></div></section>
