<section><h3 id="http--requestsocket"><code>request.socket</code><a id="http_request_socket"></a></h3><div class="api_metadata">Added in: v0.3.0</div><ul>
<li>Type: <a href="#class-streamduplex">stream.Duplex</a></li>
</ul><p>Reference to the underlying socket. Usually users will not want to access
this property. In particular, the socket will not emit <code>'readable'</code> events
because of how the protocol parser attaches to the socket.</p><div class="codeblock"><p class="codelabel">ES module version:</p><pre><code>import http from 'node:http';
const options = {
  host: 'www.google.com',
};
const req = http.get(options);
req.end();
req.once('response', (res) => {
  const ip = req.socket.localAddress;
  const port = req.socket.localPort;
  console.log(`Your IP address is ${ip} and your source port is ${port}.`);
  // Consume response object
});</code></pre><p class="codeend">End of code.</p><p class="codelabel">CommonJS version:</p><pre><code>const http = require('node:http');
const options = {
  host: 'www.google.com',
};
const req = http.get(options);
req.end();
req.once('response', (res) => {
  const ip = req.socket.localAddress;
  const port = req.socket.localPort;
  console.log(`Your IP address is ${ip} and your source port is ${port}.`);
  // Consume response object
});</code></pre><p class="codeend">End of code.</p></div><p>This property is guaranteed to be an instance of the <a href="#class-netsocket">net.Socket</a> class,
a subclass of <a href="#class-streamduplex">stream.Duplex</a>, unless the user specified a socket
type other than <a href="#class-netsocket">net.Socket</a>.</p></section>
